# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere, in the index AND in the working tree.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set locally and this repository carried no
# `.gitattributes`, so every text file was rewritten to CRLF in the working
# tree on checkout. That is harmless for almost everything here: DC-1
# normalises CRLF to LF before hashing, so every digest, seal, registry row and
# approval binding is immune to it by construction.
#
# It is NOT harmless in two places.
#
# 1. A generated artifact compared byte-for-byte.
#    `params/generated/SEWCP-200.csv` is emitted with LF endings and no
#    byte-order mark and is imported directly by Fusion 360 at CAD package step
#    6.02. A stash/checkout round-trip rewrote it to CRLF and broke
#    `python -m aief_params check`, which is how this file first came to exist.
#
# 2. Check V-25, which reads the WORKING TREE and not the index: "Every text
#    artifact under .ai/ and every artifact registered in project/FROZEN.md ...
#    uses LF line endings exclusively". A checkout under `core.autocrlf=true`
#    therefore breaks V-25 by construction, and V-25 is an AMD-31 compile-time
#    precondition of Compiler Stage 6. This is exactly the exposure recorded at
#    OI-V-05 FIND-Q3-4 ("a future checkout may rewrite working-tree files to
#    CRLF and break the literal V-25 working-tree condition; a `.gitattributes`
#    declaring LF for `.ai/**` is a `repository-engineer` action"), and it was
#    realised: 62 files carried CR at the start of session S-2026-08-11-06.
#
# WHY ONE BLANKET RULE RATHER THAN A LIST OF PARTITIONS. The first repair here
# declared `.ai/**`, `spec/**`, `framework/**` and `*.py` - the V-25 domain plus
# the tooling. That left `cad/**`, `releases/**`, `implementation/**` and
# `ENGINEERING.md` under autocrlf, and 48 tracked files were still CRLF in the
# working tree immediately after the repair. Enumerating partitions means the
# next partition is missed the same way.
#
# The blanket rule is safe here because it was checked rather than assumed:
# EVERY tracked file in this repository decodes as UTF-8 and contains no NUL
# octet - 232 `.md`, 91 `.py`, 79 `.gitkeep`, 69 `.json`, one `.csv`, one
# `.manifest`, and four extensionless text artifacts (`.ai/core/VERSION`,
# `.ai/core/validation/MANIFEST`, `.ai/project/ledger/HEAD`, `LICENSE`). There
# is no binary content to damage. Re-run that check before adding any.
#
# `text` is stated explicitly rather than left to `text=auto` heuristics, so
# the outcome does not depend on git's content sniffing.
#
# No registered digest moves under any of this: DC-1 normalises CRLF to LF
# before hashing, so it is a working-tree and index-normalisation repair only.
# Verified by comparing the DC-1 of every affected file before and after.

* text eol=lf

# Kept explicit for the two cases whose reasons are recorded above, so that
# deleting the blanket rule does not silently un-fix them.
params/generated/*.csv text eol=lf
.gitattributes         text eol=lf
